  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Which of the following triangles cannot exist?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "An acute isosceles triangle",
    "B": "An isosceles right triangle",
    "C": "An obtuse right triangle",
    "D": "A scalene right triangle",
    "E": "A scalene obtuse triangle"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A right triangle has one \\(90^\\circ\\) angle. Its other two positive angles must therefore add to \\(90^\\circ\\), so each is acute. Thus a right triangle cannot also have an obtuse angle.\n\nThe other combinations do occur. For A, B, D, and E, respective examples of angle triples are \\((70^\\circ,70^\\circ,40^\\circ)\\), \\((45^\\circ,45^\\circ,90^\\circ)\\), \\((30^\\circ,60^\\circ,90^\\circ)\\), and \\((20^\\circ,40^\\circ,120^\\circ)\\). Equal angles give equal opposite sides, while three different angles give three different sides.\n\nThe triangle that cannot exist is an obtuse right triangle, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
